        "metadata": {
            "kira_final": "Done. The compiler builds fully (`world` and `opt.opt`) and the basic testsuite passes cleanly: **40/40 tests passed, 0 failed**.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe bug was in `pool_sweep()` in `runtime/shared_heap.c` — the new run-length-compressed free-list sweep had two pointer-advancement bugs that corrupted the pool scan:\n\n1. **Free blocks were double-counted.** A free header with wosize `n` covers itself plus `n` following blocks. The code did `p += wh * Wosize_hd(hd)` (skipping the followers) and then fell through to `p += Whsize_hd(hd)`, which for a free header is `n+1` words — advancing `n+1` extra words past the whole run and landing mid-block on garbage.\n   *Fix:* advance by the full run `p += wh * (Wosize_hd(hd) + 1)` and `continue`.\n\n2. **Live blocks didn't consume their full size-class slot.** The fall-through path advanced only `p += Whsize_hd(hd)` (the object's actual size), ignoring the padding to `wh`, so the scan drifted off the block grid whenever a live object was smaller than its size-class slot.\n   *Fix:* advance by the full slot width `p += wh` for live blocks.\n\nBoth fixes match the (correct) traversal pattern already used in `calc_pool_stats()`. With these changes, the bootstrap segfault in `camlinternalFormatBasics.cmi` is gone, a debug-runtime (`ocamlrund`) heap-verification assertion (`p == end`) no longer fires, and `make -C testsuite one DIR=tests/basic` reports 40 passed / 0 failed.",
            "session_id": "tb-9a7a8af707"
        }
